  "body": "# Problem - Abbreviations are Not Semantic\r\n\r\nWhen HTML was first introduced, a number of elements were created with abbreviated tags and abbreviated attributes. As HTML has grown, more elements have been added with tags attributes focused on being semantic. These new tags and attributes are no longer abbreviations, but full words that are very descriptive of what they represent.\r\n\r\nElements like `<article>`, `<footer>`, and `<main>` would not be the same if they had been `<art>`, `<foo>`, and `<m>`. Abbreviations are much less semantic. Additionally, abbreviations add an extra layer of difficulty and confusion for new developers. Many of my students often try to use `<image source>` or `<paragraph>` because that makes more sense than `<img src>` and `<p>`.\r\n\r\nThat said, abbreviated tags are all over the web, so they can't just be swapped over as that would break too many things.\r\n\r\n# Solution - Alias Tags and Alias Attributes\r\n\r\nAs such, semantic aliases should be implemented that could be added to future sites or updated on existing sites. These aliases would NOT replace current tags nor attributes. They would only exist as an option for developers who wish to use them.\r\n\r\nA few example aliases are included below.\r\n\r\n## Tag Examples\r\n\r\n<table>\r\n<thead>\r\n\t<tr>\r\n\t\t<th>Tag</th>\r\n\t\t<th>Alias Tag</th>\r\n\t</tr>\r\n</thead>\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>img\r\n\t<td>image\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>p\r\n\t<td>paragraph\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>pre\r\n\t<td>preformat\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>br\r\n\t<td>break\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>h1\r\n\t<td>header1\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>q\r\n\t<td>quote\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n## Attribute Examples\r\n\r\n<table>\r\n<thead>\r\n\t<tr>\r\n\t\t<th>Attribute</th>\r\n\t\t<th>Alias Alias</th>\r\n\t</tr>\r\n</thead>\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>src\r\n\t<td>source\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>dir\r\n\t<td>direction\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>lang\r\n\t<td>language\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>csp\r\n\t<td>securitypolicy\r\n<tr>\r\n\t<td>alt\r\n\t<td>readertext\r\n</table>",
